  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Green Batik Workshop</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Batik is an icon of Indonesian culture that developed in the 17th century. Initially started as a pastime activity, the craftsmanship of batik painting has been passed down for generations.While batik is produced across the Indonesian archipelago, the city of Pekalongan on the north coast of Central Java stands out as the undisputed &#x2018;Capital of Batik&#x2019;, contributes to fulfilling more than 70% of domestic demand which is marketed to all regions in Indonesia.Unfortunately, anyone visiting Pekalongan today will immediately see the &#x2018;ugly side&#x2019; of batik: traditional production methods are causing huge environmental damage in the rivers, and dangerous labour conditions harm workers&#x2019; health.Building on the activities in Pekalongan, together with the Dutch Government, a visual artist &amp; batik researcher, and The Water Agency is organising the Green Batik Workshop that serves as a forward-looking forum to discuss actions and explore collaboration from diverse areas such as wastewater treatment, sustainable trade, city branding, culture preservation, sustainable energy, circular water, waste management, and other areas.</narrative>
  </description>
